WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.663
Lesson two is structured output and constrained decoding.

2
00:00:03.993 --> 00:00:07.869
Agents need values that always parse, not prose that usually does.

3
00:00:08.669 --> 00:00:12.352
Picture an expense bot that files receipts into a finance system.

4
00:00:12.682 --> 00:00:16.301
That system accepts exactly twelve categories, and nothing else.

5
00:00:17.101 --> 00:00:21.041
Now and then, the bot invents a thirteenth category, like team vibes.

6
00:00:21.371 --> 00:00:23.902
The import rejects it, and the whole batch fails.

7
00:00:24.702 --> 00:00:27.703
Asking for JSON in the prompt guarantees nothing.

8
00:00:28.033 --> 00:00:31.064
Validating and retrying usually works eventually,

9
00:00:31.114 --> 00:00:34.020
but costs extra round trips and can still fail.

10
00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:38.695
Provider JSON or schema mode is the common production default.

11
00:00:39.025 --> 00:00:43.007
It returns valid JSON matching your schema, at no extra cost.

12
00:00:43.807 --> 00:00:46.531
Constrained decoding goes further: at every step,

13
00:00:46.581 --> 00:00:49.475
it masks out any token that would break the grammar.

14
00:00:49.805 --> 00:00:53.189
Invalid output becomes impossible, not just unlikely.

15
00:00:53.989 --> 00:00:59.213
NVIDIA's stack supports this as guided decoding in TensorRT-LLM and NIM,

16
00:00:59.263 --> 00:01:02.217
with backends like XGrammar and Outlines.

17
00:01:02.547 --> 00:01:06.123
With an enum of the twelve categories, a thirteenth can never appear.

18
00:01:06.923 --> 00:01:08.865
Next, schema-first development:

19
00:01:08.915 --> 00:01:12.463
define the output once, for example as a Pydantic model.

20
00:01:12.793 --> 00:01:15.872
That one definition produces the schema for the model,

21
00:01:15.922 --> 00:01:19.292
validates what comes back, and types everything downstream.

22
00:01:20.092 --> 00:01:24.245
Make escalation a field the model fills in, like requires human review.

23
00:01:24.575 --> 00:01:26.765
A true or false beats string-matching prose.

24
00:01:27.565 --> 00:01:30.843
Field order matters too: put the rationale before the verdict.

25
00:01:31.173 --> 00:01:33.893
Then the model reasons first and decides second,

26
00:01:33.943 --> 00:01:36.605
instead of justifying a choice it already made.

27
00:01:37.405 --> 00:01:39.814
Use enums for anything you'll branch on,

28
00:01:39.864 --> 00:01:44.609
and include an explicit unsure option, or the schema forces a confident guess.

29
00:01:44.939 --> 00:01:48.601
And keep schemas shallow, because deep nesting raises the error rate.

30
00:01:49.401 --> 00:01:52.593
Remember, structured output constrains form, not truth.

31
00:01:52.923 --> 00:01:56.982
A perfectly valid record can still file a taxi ride as catering,

32
00:01:57.032 --> 00:01:59.935
so correctness needs grounding and evaluation.

33
00:02:00.735 --> 00:02:02.199
A classic exam question:

34
00:02:02.249 --> 00:02:06.615
an agent must pick one of fifty tool names, and sometimes invents one.

35
00:02:06.945 --> 00:02:10.882
Retries, lower temperature, and better prompts only reduce that;

36
00:02:10.932 --> 00:02:13.871
constrained decoding over an enum eliminates it.

37
00:02:14.671 --> 00:02:18.184
Unlikely versus impossible is the distinction to remember.

38
00:02:18.514 --> 00:02:22.261
Read on for the full comparison table and the schema design rules.
